@page "/about"

<PageTitle>About BasketElo</PageTitle>

<div class="about-page be-story">
    <header class="be-story-hero be-reveal" style="--reveal-order: 0">
        <div class="be-story-hero-copy">
            <p class="be-story-kicker">A small project with a large map</p>
            <h1 class="be-story-title">Built from curiosity, one result at a time.</h1>
            <p class="be-story-lede">BasketElo began with a question I kept returning to: could a chess idea help us see basketball differently?</p>
        </div>
        <div class="be-story-orbit" aria-hidden="true">
            <div class="be-story-orbit-core">
                <span>Independent project</span>
                <strong>1 → many</strong>
                <small>one builder, many leagues</small>
            </div>
        </div>
    </header>

    <section class="be-story-section be-reveal" style="--reveal-order: 1" aria-labelledby="story-heading">
        <div class="be-story-meta"><span class="be-story-index">01</span><span>The origin</span></div>
        <div class="be-story-body">
            <h2 id="story-heading" class="be-story-heading">An engineer, a dataset, and a lot of curiosity.</h2>
            <div class="be-story-copy about-copy-columns">
                <p>I'm an engineer who has always been curious about ELO in chess: how a simple number can absorb thousands of games and still give us a changing picture of strength. I wanted to understand what would happen when that idea met basketball, where margins, home courts, leagues, seasons, and international competition add their own texture.</p>
                <p>BasketElo began as a hobby project to compare European leagues. It was a way to learn, tinker, and answer my own questions with data rather than instinct.</p>
                <p>It has since grown to include the NBA and international basketball, partly because the questions kept getting more interesting—and partly because modern tools make it possible for one person to build, test, and improve an ambitious idea.</p>
            </div>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="be-story-section be-reveal" style="--reveal-order: 2" aria-labelledby="values-heading">
        <div class="be-story-meta"><span class="be-story-index">02</span><span>Working principles</span></div>
        <div class="be-story-body">
            <h2 id="values-heading" class="be-story-heading">Curiosity should leave a trail.</h2>
            <div class="about-principles">
                <article><span>01</span><div><h3>Show the work</h3><p>Ratings are more useful when people can understand where they came from and question the assumptions behind them.</p></div></article>
                <article><span>02</span><div><h3>Stay evidence-led</h3><p>Results are noisy. The goal is not to pretend otherwise, but to make the signal easier to inspect and explore.</p></div></article>
                <article><span>03</span><div><h3>Keep learning in public</h3><p>Rulesets can improve, data coverage can expand, and the project can change its mind when the evidence earns it.</p></div></article>
            </div>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="about-field-note be-reveal" style="--reveal-order: 3" aria-labelledby="why-heading">
        <div class="about-field-note-label"><span>03</span><span>Field note / Why continue?</span></div>
        <div>
            <h2 id="why-heading">“Because the game is bigger than one table.”</h2>
            <p>There is something satisfying about putting different competitions on the same map while respecting their differences. BasketElo is my ongoing attempt to make that exploration clear, useful, and a little more fun.</p>
            <nav aria-label="Continue exploring">
                <a href="rankings">Explore the rankings <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a>
                <a href="how-it-works">Read the method <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a>
            </nav>
        </div>
        <p class="about-signoff">Built as a hobby project<br><strong>with data, patience &amp; curiosity.</strong></p>
    </section>
</div>
